Bulletin boards, an overview.
============================

Introduction
------------

I wont go into the details of what the boards do, except to
note that these boards can communicate across muds. ie, it is
possible to configure them so that if you post a note on
one mud and it can appear of a board on a different mud.

Main Files
----------

/include/board.h
	Gives the file positions of the important board files. You should
	include this if you are going to use a board.
	
/RO/remote/rboard.c  (use: RBOARD_SERV)
  The remote board manager. It implements a protocol to
  keep all of the distributed boards in sync, and is able
  to bring any boards that have missed a message (for whatever
  reason) up to date. Since its based on datagrams the 
  protocol is rather loose.

/RO/remote/remote_boards   
	Specification file for which of the local boards are distributed.
	If your board isnt mentioned here then it will be treated as
	a stand alone object.
	
/obj/bulletin/board_serv (use: BOARD_SERV) 
	Virtual board manager. This object is used when a board is 
	first referenced to clone up the board and rename it.
	The board is renamed as BOARD_SERV + "/" + board_name.
	Since this code uses the virtual directory abilities it
	allows you to code
	      board = load_object (BOARD_SERV + "/" + board_name);
	to find/load a board. DO NOT clone a board directly.
	
/obj/bulletin/bboard (use: BBOARD)
	Main bulletin board file. There are many comments at the top
	of this object to detail its use.


Adding a New Board
------------------

To add a new board to the mud:

	a) Add the boards name to the board server (BOARD_SERV).
  b) Write a room to contain it.
  c) add in the line 
  
             #include <board.h>
      at the top of the file.       
  d) add in the code below to the reset() function.      
  
             board = load_object (BOARD_SERV + "/" + board_name);
             /* set up */
             board -> set_short (a short desc of the board);
             board -> set_expiry (time in seconds before a note expires);
             board -> set_archive_file (file);
             /* move the the room */
             move_object (board,this_object());

     Each of the set up options have a default, and so are not
     necessary for many cases. If set_archive_file () is called
     with a zero then the expired notes are discarded.

	e) If the board is to be a distributed one then you need to 
	   add it to the file /RO/remote/remote_boards.



